+### Hierarchical Geometric Guidance
+A volume sampling strategy incorporating sparse depth priors from Structure from Motion (SfM) into scene representations by sampling volume points in a local-to-global manner [S4, unofficial]. It initializes sampling around the depth prior in a local region and gradually extends to full scene bounds, mitigating geometric misalignment caused by depth prior bias [S4, unofficial].
+
+### Pose-Related Shading Factor
+Decomposes radiance color into pose-independent RGB color $c_0$ and pose-dependent shading factor $s$ to handle bent rays and self-occlusion in posed humans [S5, unofficial]. The shared color and feature vector are predicted by an appearance network, while a shallow shading network estimates $s$ conditioned on the target pose $\theta_t$, yielding $c = c_0 s$ [S5, unofficial].
+
+### Density Field Prediction
+An MLP $F_\sigma$ that predicts density $\sigma$ using an interpolated geometry latent code $l_{geo}$ and signed height indicator $\tilde{h}$ [S5, unofficial]. The density is computed as $\sigma = F_\sigma(\phi(\tilde{h}), l_{geo})$, where $\phi(\cdot)$ is the position encoding function [S5, unofficial].
+
+### Neural Appearance Blending
+A neural blending scheme for appearance refinement that recovers texture information from two adjacent input views to address artifacts from sparse inputs [S6]. It back-projects points from a target view depth map into neighboring views, extracts features and visibility, and feeds them into a blending network to compute weights $W_q = \mathbf{MLP}_\mathcal{A}(f^{\mathbf{v}_1}_q, O^{\mathbf{v}_1}_q, f^{\mathbf{v}_2}_q, O^{\mathbf{v}_2}_q)$, yielding $C^{v*}_q = W_q \cdot C_q$ [S6].
+
+### Two-layer Volumetric Rendering
+Splits the model into two layers with separate density and appearance modules for the object and environment to allow foreground extraction and support effects like translucency [S7, unofficial]. Sample points inside a 3D bounding box use the foreground layer, outside use the background layer. Total density is $\sigma_i = \sigma_{io} + \sigma_{ie}$, and color is $c_i = (\sigma_{io} c_{oi} + \sigma_{ie} c_{ei}) / \max(\sigma_i, \epsilon)$ [S7, unofficial].
+
+### Grid-guided Neural Radiance Field
+A mechanism where the NeRF branch provides global regularization and pointwise guidance to refine independently optimized grid features, addressing noisy artifacts and lack of space continuity in grid-based methods [S8]. The grid branch enriches scene information via reconstruction loss while the NeRF branch focuses on missing high-frequency details, allowing joint refinement rather than freezing grids [S8].
+
+### Multi-view Distillation Optimization
+An optimization strategy using multi-view distillation information to guide training of the NDRF, suppressing artifacts and approximating original NeRF rendering quality [S9]. It renders views from distillation cameras using the original NeRF to obtain targets, renders from training cameras using NDRF, and optimizes via render loss [S9].
 
 ## Background
-Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) were introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1, S3]. The method represents a continuous volumetric scene representation using a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) that maps 3D spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and emitted color [S1, S3]. This approach solved the problem of high-quality novel view synthesis from a sparse set of 2D images by leveraging differentiable volume rendering to train the implicit representation [S1, S2]. Prior to NeRF, explicit representations like voxel grids or meshes required significant memory or manual geometry processing, while previous implicit methods lacked the capacity to model complex view-dependent effects [S1][S4, unofficial]. NeRF's use of positional encoding and hierarchical sampling allowed it to capture high-frequency details and realistic lighting, setting a new standard for neural rendering [S1, S3]. Since its introduction, the field has expanded into dynamic scenes, large-scale environments, and efficient training/inference techniques [S1, S2].
+Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) were introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1, S3]. The method represents a continuous volumetric scene representation using a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) that maps 3D spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and emitted color [S1, S3]. This approach solved the problem of high-quality novel view synthesis from a sparse set of 2D images by leveraging differentiable volume rendering to train the implicit representation [S1, S2]. Prior to NeRF, explicit representations like voxel grids or meshes required significant memory or manual geometry processing, while previous implicit methods lacked the capacity to model complex view-dependent effects [S1][S4, unofficial]. NeRF's use of positional encoding and hierarchical sampling allowed it to capture high-frequency details and realistic lighting, setting a new standard for neural rendering [S1, S3].
 
